          }
        ],
        "author": {
          "name": "Hiroshi Nishida",
          "email": "hnishida@thelustrecollective.com",
          "date": "2026-10-30 03:03:29.000000000",
          "tz": -420
        },
        "committer": {
          "name": "Hiroshi Nishida",
          "email": "hnishida@thelustrecollective.com",
          "date": "2026-11-02 22:16:50.000000000",
          "tz": -480
        },
        "subject": "LU-21089 utils: add LVM thin backend to lctl snapshot",
        "message": "LU-21089 utils: add LVM thin backend to lctl snapshot\n\nlctl snapshot_* works only for ZFS targets. An ldiskfs target on\nan LVM thin LV can be snapshotted the same way: dm suspend freezes\nthe filesystem, so a thin snapshot taken under the write barrier\nis a clean image. dm-thin copies a block once per snapshot\ngeneration, so the cost does not grow with the snapshot count, as\nit does with classic LVM snapshots.\n\nAccept \"lvm:/dev/<vg>/<lv>\" devices in ldev.conf. The snapshot\npiece is the read-only thin LV <lv>_ss_<ssname>, and the lustre:*\nZFS properties become lustre_* LV tags, with the comment\nhex-encoded. Nothing is written into a piece: it is mounted with\nrdonly_dev (LU-15873) and fsname=<snapshot fsname>.\n\nUnlike ZFS, an LVM piece is made on the node where the origin LV\nis active, with no retry on the failover node, and a target with\nan external journal is refused. Pieces keep the LVM activation\nskip flag, so they are active only while mounted.\n\nLet sanity-lsnapshot run on ldiskfs when every target is an LVM\nthin LV, with FLAKEY=false. CI has no such targets, so CI only\nchecks that the ZFS path is unchanged.\n\nTest-Parameters: trivial fstype=zfs testlist=sanity-lsnapshot\nSigned-off-by: Hiroshi Nishida <hnishida@thelustrecollective.com>\nAssisted-by: ClaudeCode:claude-opus-5-5\nChange-Id: Id6768dca224cc5b788c9a084fd20995fe882ca70\n"
      },
      "branch": "refs/heads/master"
    }
